      "side": 1,
      "message": "This will mean that whevener the active request for an NRI changes, we\u0027ll send callback for a different request. Look at ConnectivityManager.java:3683 : the other end is looking up the request in a map. This will call missed callbacks and a lot of logs.\n\nReally the more I think about it the less I see how we can make sense of callbacks for multilayer requests without some way of identifying the NRI. For applications, the NRI is what they call a \"request\". I think the only way to really fix it is to have a relatively big refactoring step where an NRI is called a \"request\", what we call NetworkRequest is called, say, layer or something, and we make sure the callbacks are called for the right requests.\n\nThankfully the only user of multilayer NRIs won\u0027t actually use the callbacks and there is no public API to file a multilayer request that results in callbacks. That means I\u0027ll take on the refactoring next year when I have time. Little by little.\n\nImmediately, we have the choice between sending these callbacks (which nobody listens to) to a NetworkRequest that was never registered (which is what PS22 is doing) or to a NetworkRequest that it doesn\u0027t satisfy (which is what we\u0027d do if we\u0027d always use nri.mRequests.get(0)).\n\nConsidering the request in the return path from the system server to the app is hidden away inside ConnectivityManager, I think the latter is a better solution. I think we should simply always use nri.mRequests.get(0) here instead. Add appropriate comments, e.g. :\n\n  // In the case of multi-layer NRIs, the first request is not necessarily the one that\n  // is satisfied. This is vexing, but the ConnectivityManager code that receives this\n  // callback is only using the request as a token to identify the callback, so it doesn\u0027t\n  // matter too much at this point as long as the callback can be found.\n  // TODO : make sure callbacks are indexed by NRIs instead of NetworkRequest objects.",
